    "lawCode": "Add §125, Ag & Mkts L",
    "lawSection": "Agriculture and Markets Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5354\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the agriculture and markets law, in\nrelation to requiring the promulgation and implementation of standards\nfor the designation of therapy dog\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1: Sets forth that the State Commissioner of Agriculture and\nMarkets create standards and regulations for training, evaluation, and\ncertification required for designating dogs as \"therapy dogs\". It also\nrequires that said standards be established by no later than October\n1, 2017.\n\nSection 2: Establishes the effective date as immediately.\n\n CURRENT LAW :\n\nSection 108 of the Agriculture and Markets Law defines \"Therapy dog\"\nas any dog that is trained to aid the emotional and physical health of\npatients in hospitals, nursing homes, retirement homes and other\nsettings and is actually used for such purpose, or any dog during the\nperiod such dog is being trained or bred for such purpose.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThough they serve an increasingly important role in society, there is\nno presently defined process for certifying therapy dogs in the State\nof New York. Therapy dogs do not fall under State or Federal (through\nthe Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990) law as \"service dogs\",\nwhich have certain protections and qualifications.\n\nCurrent law defines a therapy dog as a dog \"trained...for such\npurpose\" as providing for emotional and physical health, but does not\nset forth any training or credentialing requirements. As such, any dog\nmay be described as a \"therapy dog\". regardless of whether or not they\nhave received proper training. This presents situations wherein\nuntrained animals presented as so-called therapy dogs can be in the\npresence of the public and other, trained, service animals. There have\nbeen instances of trained service animals being maimed by untrained\nanimals. By requiring that formal standards for training and\ncredentialing be established, the State can not only ensure the safety\nof the public, but it can also ensure that those who would be served\nby therapy dogs will best be served through the use of trained and\ncredentialed animals.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nThis is a new bill:\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
